<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" standalone="yes"?><rss version="2.0" xmlns:atom="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom"><channel><title>Single Board Computers on Pi4J</title><link>https://www.pi4j.com/sbc/</link><description>Recent content in Single Board Computers on Pi4J</description><generator>Hugo</generator><language>en-us</language><atom:link href="https://www.pi4j.com/sbc/index.xml" rel="self" type="application/rss+xml"/><item><title>x86 vs ARM vs RISC-V</title><link>https://www.pi4j.com/sbc/x86-arm-riscv/</link><pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 0001 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://www.pi4j.com/sbc/x86-arm-riscv/</guid><description>&lt;p&gt;This is an overview of the different processor types used in computers, so also in Single Board Computers (SBCs).&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;h2 id="arm-efficient-by-design"&gt;ARM: Efficient by Design&lt;/h2&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arm_Holdings" target="_blank" rel="noopener"&gt;Arm Holdings plc&lt;/a&gt; (originally &amp;ldquo;Acorn RISC Machine&amp;rdquo;, later &amp;ldquo;Advanced RISC Machines&amp;rdquo;) is a British semiconductor and software design company. There primary business is the design of central processing unit (CPU) cores that implement the ARM architecture family of instruction sets. They don&amp;rsquo;t manufacture the chips, but design the architecture and license it to others. This means that an application written for a &lt;a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Qualcomm_Snapdragon" target="_blank" rel="noopener"&gt;Qualcomm Snapdragon&lt;/a&gt; processor should also work on a &lt;a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Exynos" target="_blank" rel="noopener"&gt;Samsung Exynos&lt;/a&gt; or &lt;a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apple_M1" target="_blank" rel="noopener"&gt;Apple&amp;rsquo;s M-series&lt;/a&gt; chip because they speak the same &amp;ldquo;ARM language&amp;rdquo;.&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>Elecrow CrowPi</title><link>https://www.pi4j.com/sbc/crowpi/</link><pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 0001 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://www.pi4j.com/sbc/crowpi/</guid><description>&lt;p&gt;The &lt;a href="https://www.elecrow.com/crowpi.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener"&gt;CrowPi is an electronics starter kit in a laptop-housing&lt;/a&gt;.&#10;Of course, you can get any electronics starter kit for this purpose or buy separate components. That&amp;rsquo;s the fun of&#10;electronics: components are inexpensive and easy to find on ebay or one of the many web shops.&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;&#10;&#10;&#10;&lt;div class="gallery caption-position-bottom caption-effect-slide hover-effect-zoom hover-transition" itemscope itemtype="http://schema.org/ImageGallery"&gt;&#10;&#9; &#10;&#10;&lt;link rel="stylesheet" href="https://www.pi4j.com/css/hugo-easy-gallery.css" /&gt;&#10;&lt;div class="box fancy-figure caption-position-center caption-effect-fade" &gt;&#10; &lt;figure itemprop="associatedMedia" itemscope itemtype="http://schema.org/ImageObject"&gt;&#10; &lt;div class="img" style="background-image: url('/assets/examples/crowpi/crowpi1.jpg');"&gt;&#10; &lt;img itemprop="thumbnail" src="https://www.pi4j.com/assets/examples/crowpi/crowpi1.jpg" alt="CrowPi 1"/&gt;&#10; &lt;/div&gt;&#10; &lt;a href="https://www.pi4j.com/assets/examples/crowpi/crowpi1.jpg" itemprop="contentUrl"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&#10; &lt;figcaption&gt;&#10; &lt;p&gt;CrowPi 1&lt;/p&gt;&#10; &lt;/figcaption&gt;&#10; &lt;/figure&gt;&#10;&lt;/div&gt;&#10;&#10;&#10;&#10;&lt;div class="box fancy-figure caption-position-center caption-effect-fade" &gt;&#10; &lt;figure itemprop="associatedMedia" itemscope itemtype="http://schema.org/ImageObject"&gt;&#10; &lt;div class="img" style="background-image: url('/assets/examples/crowpi/crowpi2.jpg');"&gt;&#10; &lt;img itemprop="thumbnail" src="https://www.pi4j.com/assets/examples/crowpi/crowpi2.jpg" alt="CrowPi 2"/&gt;&#10; &lt;/div&gt;&#10; &lt;a href="https://www.pi4j.com/assets/examples/crowpi/crowpi2.jpg" itemprop="contentUrl"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&#10; &lt;figcaption&gt;&#10; &lt;p&gt;CrowPi 2&lt;/p&gt;&#10; &lt;/figcaption&gt;&#10; &lt;/figure&gt;&#10;&lt;/div&gt;&#10;&#10;&#10;&#10;&lt;div class="box fancy-figure caption-position-center caption-effect-fade" &gt;&#10; &lt;figure itemprop="associatedMedia" itemscope itemtype="http://schema.org/ImageObject"&gt;&#10; &lt;div class="img" style="background-image: url('/assets/examples/crowpi/electronics-starter-kit.jpg');"&gt;&#10; &lt;img itemprop="thumbnail" src="https://www.pi4j.com/assets/examples/crowpi/electronics-starter-kit.jpg" alt="Example electronics starter kit"/&gt;&#10; &lt;/div&gt;&#10; &lt;a href="https://www.pi4j.com/assets/examples/crowpi/electronics-starter-kit.jpg" itemprop="contentUrl"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&#10; &lt;figcaption&gt;&#10; &lt;p&gt;Example electronics starter kit&lt;/p&gt;&#10; &lt;/figcaption&gt;&#10; &lt;/figure&gt;&#10;&lt;/div&gt;&#10;&#10;&#10;&lt;/div&gt;&#10;&#10;&#10;&#10;&#10;&#10; &#10;&#10;&#10;&lt;script src="https://www.pi4j.com/js/load-photoswipe.js"&gt;&lt;/script&gt;&#10;&#10;&#10;&lt;link rel="stylesheet" href="https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/photoswipe/4.1.1/photoswipe.min.css" integrity="sha256-sCl5PUOGMLfFYctzDW3MtRib0ctyUvI9Qsmq2wXOeBY=" crossorigin="anonymous" /&gt;&#10;&lt;link rel="stylesheet" href="https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/photoswipe/4.1.1/default-skin/default-skin.min.css" integrity="sha256-BFeI1V+Vh1Rk37wswuOYn5lsTcaU96hGaI7OUVCLjPc=" crossorigin="anonymous" /&gt;&#10;&lt;script src="https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/photoswipe/4.1.1/photoswipe.min.js" integrity="sha256-UplRCs9v4KXVJvVY+p+RSo5Q4ilAUXh7kpjyIP5odyc=" crossorigin="anonymous"&gt;&lt;/script&gt;&#10;&lt;script src="https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/photoswipe/4.1.1/photoswipe-ui-default.min.js" integrity="sha256-PWHOlUzc96pMc8ThwRIXPn8yH4NOLu42RQ0b9SpnpFk=" crossorigin="anonymous"&gt;&lt;/script&gt;&#10;&#10;&#10;&lt;div class="pswp" tabindex="-1" role="dialog" aria-hidden="true"&gt;&#10;&#10;&lt;div class="pswp__bg"&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&#10;&#10;&lt;div class="pswp__scroll-wrap"&gt;&#10; &#10; &lt;div class="pswp__container"&gt;&#10; &lt;div class="pswp__item"&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&#10; &lt;div class="pswp__item"&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&#10; &lt;div class="pswp__item"&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&#10; &lt;/div&gt;&#10; &#10; &lt;div class="pswp__ui pswp__ui--hidden"&gt;&#10; &lt;div class="pswp__top-bar"&gt;&#10; &#10; &lt;div class="pswp__counter"&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&#10; &lt;button class="pswp__button pswp__button--close" title="Close (Esc)"&gt;&lt;/button&gt;&#10; &lt;button class="pswp__button pswp__button--share" title="Share"&gt;&lt;/button&gt;&#10; &lt;button class="pswp__button pswp__button--fs" title="Toggle fullscreen"&gt;&lt;/button&gt;&#10; &lt;button class="pswp__button pswp__button--zoom" title="Zoom in/out"&gt;&lt;/button&gt;&#10; &#10; &#10; &lt;div class="pswp__preloader"&gt;&#10; &lt;div class="pswp__preloader__icn"&gt;&#10; &lt;div class="pswp__preloader__cut"&gt;&#10; &lt;div class="pswp__preloader__donut"&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&#10; &lt;/div&gt;&#10; &lt;/div&gt;&#10; &lt;/div&gt;&#10; &lt;/div&gt;&#10; &lt;div class="pswp__share-modal pswp__share-modal--hidden pswp__single-tap"&gt;&#10; &lt;div class="pswp__share-tooltip"&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&#10; &lt;/div&gt;&#10; &lt;button class="pswp__button pswp__button--arrow--left" title="Previous (arrow left)"&gt;&#10; &lt;/button&gt;&#10; &lt;button class="pswp__button pswp__button--arrow--right" title="Next (arrow right)"&gt;&#10; &lt;/button&gt;&#10; &lt;div class="pswp__caption"&gt;&#10; &lt;div class="pswp__caption__center"&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&#10; &lt;/div&gt;&#10; &lt;/div&gt;&#10; &lt;/div&gt;&#10;&lt;/div&gt;&#10;&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>Using Pi4J on Other Brands</title><link>https://www.pi4j.com/sbc/using-pi4j-on-other-brands/</link><pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 0001 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://www.pi4j.com/sbc/using-pi4j-on-other-brands/</guid><description>&lt;p&gt;Pi4J started with, and is best documented for, the Raspberry Pi. On a Raspberry Pi, the GPIO numbers you pass to Pi4J match the SoC&amp;rsquo;s own &amp;ldquo;BCM&amp;rdquo; numbering directly, so pin 16 on the header is BCM 23 as you can see in the GPIO header for, for instance, the &lt;a href="https://api.pi4j.com/board-information/MODEL_5_B" target="_blank" rel="noopener"&gt;Raspberry Pi 5&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;Many other SBCs (Banana Pi, Orange Pi, and other Allwinner- or Rockchip-based boards) use a different SoC with its own GPIO controller and numbering scheme, even when the board physically matches the Raspberry Pi&amp;rsquo;s header layout. This page walks through the general process of finding the right GPIO addressing for such a board, using a Banana Pi M4 Zero (Allwinner H618) as an example.&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>Setting up Pi4J for RISC-V</title><link>https://www.pi4j.com/sbc/riscv/</link><pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 0001 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://www.pi4j.com/sbc/riscv/</guid><description>&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/RISC-V" target="_blank" rel="noopener"&gt;RISC-V&lt;/a&gt; is an open, royalty-free instruction set architecture, and boards built around it are becoming more common as alternatives to ARM-based SBCs. This page collects what&amp;rsquo;s needed to get Java and Pi4J running on RISC-V hardware.&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&#10;&lt;div class="notices warning" &gt;&lt;p&gt;This page is a work in progress. Expect more tested boards and details in the near future&amp;hellip;&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;/div&gt;&#10;&#10;&lt;h2 id="tested-boards"&gt;Tested boards&lt;/h2&gt;&#10;&lt;ol&gt;&#10;&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://milkv.io/docs" target="_blank" rel="noopener"&gt;Milk-V Mars&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;&#10;&lt;/ol&gt;&#10;&lt;h2 id="installing-jdk-25"&gt;Installing JDK 25+&lt;/h2&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;There aren&amp;rsquo;t many builds of Java for the RISC-V architecture yet, so we recommend installing it from the &lt;a href="https://adoptium.net/temurin/releases" target="_blank" rel="noopener"&gt;Adoptium project&lt;/a&gt;:&lt;/p&gt;</description></item></channel></rss>